content.text:
The fix is to put the delimiter **after** each item (`::after`), not **before** the next one (`::before` or a leading span).

When an item wraps to a new line, its box moves as a whole. A **prefix** delimiter travels with it and shows at the line start. A **suffix** delimiter stays on the previous line with the item before the break.

**CSS** (`styles.css`):

```css
.headline-roles > span {
  display: inline-block;
  white-space: nowrap;
}

.headline-roles > span:not(:last-child)::after {
  content: "·";
  margin-inline: 0.75em;
}
```

**Markup** — one span per role, no delimiter elements:

```tsx
<p className="font-label-mono headline-roles mb-4 text-foreground">
  {headlineRoles.map((role) => (
    <span key={role}>{role}</span>
  ))}
</p>
```

Result on narrow widths:

```
MSC IN COMPUTER SCIENCE · SR SOFTWARE ENGINEER · SYSTEM ARCHITECT ·
AI SPECIALIST · IT CONSULTANT
```

No leading `·` on wrapped lines. You may still get a trailing `·` at the end of a line when the break falls right after an item — that’s normal with suffix separators and hard to remove in pure CSS without JavaScript.

